   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the environmental conservation law, in relation to enacting\nthe \"extended producer responsibility for artificial turf act of 2026\"",
    "basePrintNo": "S10168",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S10168-2025",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 46,
       "fullName": "Patricia Fahy",
       "imgName": "657_patricia_fahy.jpg",
       "incumbent": true,
       "memberId": 1571,
       "sessionMemberId": 2532,
       "sessionYear": 2025,
       "shortName": "FAHY"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd En Con L, generally",
    "lawSection": "Environmental Conservation Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S10168\n\nSPONSOR: KAVANAGH\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the environmental conservation law, in relation to\nenacting the \"extended producer responsibility for artificial turf act\nof 2026\"\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThis bill separates artificial turf from the definition of carpet under\nthe existing Extended Producer Responsibility for Carpets law and estab-\nlishes separate standards for collection and recycling of artificial\nturf, in recognition of distinctions between carpet and artificial turf.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one of the bill provides that this act shall be known and may be\ncited as the \"extended producer responsibility for artificial turf act\nof 2026\".\n\nSection two of the bill amends the title heading of title 33 of article\n27 of the environmental conservation law.\n\nSection three of the bill defines \"artificial turf\" and \"discarded arti-\nficial turf\" for the purposes of title 33 of article 27 of the environ-\nmental conservation law, removes artificial turf from the definition of\ncarpet, and amends the definition of \"collection site\" to include mobile\ncollection units for the purpose of collecting discarded artificial\nturf.\n\nSection four of the bill amends section 27-3303 of the environmental\nconservation law to require producer plans to include separate\ncollection convenience standards for carpet and artificial turf, includ-\ning the use of mobile collection units for artificial turf and chain-of-\ncustody requirements for transportation and disposal.\n\nSection five of the bill amends section 27-3305 of the environmental\nconservation law to require producers and representative organizations\nto implement artificial turf collection programs and comply with plan\napproval, reporting, and cost responsibility requirements.\n\nSection six of the bill amends section 27-3307 of the environmental\nconservation law to prohibit retailers from selling artificial turf\nunless the producer is participating in an approved collection program\nand allows retailers to voluntarily serve as designated collection\nsites.\n\nSection seven of the bill amends section 27-3309 of the environmental\nconservation law to apply department review, performance standards, and\npenalties for noncompliance to artificial turf collection and recycling\nprograms.\n\nSection eight of the bill amends section 27-3311 of the environmental\nconservation law to expand the carpet stewardship advisory board into\nthe carpet and artificial turf stewardship advisory board and updates\nboard representation accordingly.\n\nSection nine of the bill amends section 27-3313 of the environmental\nconservation law to apply labeling requirements and PFAS restrictions to\nartificial turf.\n\nSection ten of the bill amends section 27-3315 of the environmental\nconservation law to require artificial turf sold in the state to meet\nminimum post-consumer recycled content standards.\n\nSection eleven of the bill amends section 27-3319 of the environmental\nconservation law to extend liability protections related to antitrust,\nrestraint of trade, and unfair trade practices to artificial turf\nproducer responsibility programs.\n\nSection twelve of the bill sets forth the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nIn 2022, New York enacted extended producer responsibility (EPR) for\ncarpets, requiring producers to manage the collection, recycling, and\nend-of-life management of discarded carpet. Along with this program,\nartificial turf was included in the definition of carpet. While this\napproach ensured artificial turf was subject to EPR requirements, it did\nnot account for the difference between carpet and artificial turf in\nsize, composition, installation, removal, and collection logistics.\n\nUnlike traditional carpet, artificial turf is typically installed in\nlarge outdoor applications such as athletic fields, playgrounds, and\nlandscaping projects. It often includes infill, padding, and shock\nattenuation layers, and removal generally requires specialized equipment\nand transportation. As a result, the permanent collection site model\ndesigned for carpet is not practical for artificial turf.\n\nThis bill establishes a distinct statutory mechanism for collection and\nprocessing artificial turf within the existing EPR mandate.  Specif-\nically, it allows producers and representative organizations to utilize\nmobile collection units as the primary collection method for discarded\nartificial turf, rather than collection sites in permanent locations,\nwhile still allowing for permanent collection sites where necessary to\nensure convenience for consumers.\n\nBy recognizing the unique characteristics of artificial turf, this\nlegislation improves the effectiveness of this EPR program, increases\nthe likelihood of successful recycling and reuse, reduces improper\ndisposal, and ensures producer responsibility requirements are practical\nand enforceable.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNo significant net costs to the State. Program costs will be fully\ncovered by fees generated by the program.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
